==Specifications==
The U9 is equipped with four {{cvt|240|kW|hp PS|0}} [[electric motors]] providing a total power output of {{cvt|960|kW|hp PS|0}} and a maximum range of {{cvt|450|km}} on the [[China Light-Duty Vehicle Test Cycle]] (CLTC). BYD reported a 0–100&nbsp;km/h (62&nbsp;mph) acceleration time of 2.36 seconds, and a {{convert|1/4|mi|m|0|abbr=on}} [[drag race]] time of 9.78 seconds.

The U9 is built with an 800V electrical architecture, with a maximum [[Direct current|DC]] charging power of 500&nbsp;kW. The charging duration between 30% and 80% is 10 minutes. The U9 also supports dual charging (plugging in two chargers simultaneously) to increase charging rate.

Like the U8, the U9 features BYD's proprietary "e<sup>4</sup>" (易四方) [[individual wheel drive]] system, which enables the vehicle to redistribute [[torque]] among the four wheels in case of [[traction (engineering)|traction]] loss or a [[flat tyre|tyre puncture]] at speed; and the "DiSus" (云辇) [[active suspension]] system, which allows the wheels' [[ground clearance]] to be readjusted individually and even to perform a brief vertical jump.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2023-04-11 |title=BYD’s new supercar can jump (but not in the way you might expect) |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/byds-new-supercar-can-jump-not-way-you-might-expect |access-date=2023-11-08 |website=Top Gear |language=en}}</ref> The active suspension consists of three different systems; DiSus-C controls variable damping, DiSus-A controls the [[air suspension]], and DiSus-P controls the hydraulic system.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hundal |first=Thomas |date=2023-04-11 |title=The Electric Yangwang U9 Supercar Can Literally Jump Thanks To New Twists On Legendary Suspension Tech |url=https://www.theautopian.com/yangwang-u9-suspension/ |access-date=2024-04-13 |website=The Autopian |language=en-US}}</ref> Regarding the vertical jump, the U9 will shrink first, then bounce up, launching all four wheels off the ground at the same time.<ref name=":0" /> BYD has not announced the specific reason for the jump function on the U9, but the function demonstrates the "DiSus-X" body control system.

It was launched in 2024 with a price of 1.68 million Yuan (USD 236,000; EUR 215,000).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Opletal |first=Jiri |date=2025-01-13 |title=Police fined BYD's Yangwang U9 driver for street dance in China |url=https://carnewschina.com/2025/01/13/police-fined-byds-yangwang-u9-driver-for-street-dance/ |access-date=2025-06-06 |website=CarNewsChina.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

In August 2025, specifications of the U9 Track Edition were released by the [[Ministry of Industry and Information Technology|MIIT]]. It retains the individual wheel drive system, but changes the motors to the ''TZ240XYA'' model which output to {{cvt|555|kW|hp PS|0}} each for a total of {{cvt|2220|kW|hp PS|0}}, making it the most powerful production car ever built.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pettendy |first=Marton |date=2025-08-11 |title=BYD’s Yangwang U9 Track Edition breaks cover with an incredible 2220kW of power |url=https://www.carexpert.com.au/car-news/byds-yangwang-u9-track-edition-breaks-cover-with-an-incredible-2220kw-of-power |website=CarExpert |publisher=CarExpert |location=Australia |access-date=2025-08-12}}</ref> It is equipped with smaller 20-inch wheels with the front tire width increasing from 275{{Spaces}}mm to 325{{Spaces}}mm to match the rears.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chen |first=Hao |date=2025-08-08 |title= |script-title=zh:什么叫力大飞砖！ 3019马力的仰望U9赛道版完成工信部申报 |trans-title=What is the meaning of "powerful flying brick"! The 3019 horsepower Yangwang U9 Track Edition has completed the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology application |url=https://www.autohome.com.cn/news/202508/1308060.html#pvareaid=102624 |access-date=2025-08-08 |website=汽车之家 |language=zh-CN}}</ref> In August 2025, an engineering test sample of U9 Track Edition broke the record for the fastest top speed of an electric car, which reached {{cvt|472.41|km/h|mph}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=This Chinese BYD Electric Supercar is Now the World’s Fastest EV |url=https://www.carsauce.com/car-news/this-chinese-byd-electric-supercar-is-now-the-worlds-fastest-ev |access-date=2025-08-26 |website=www.carsauce.com |language=en-AU}}</ref>

== Records ==
On 14 September 2025, BYD officially unveiled the Yangwang U9 Xtreme, a track-focused version of the U9. It is currently the most powerful production model of the U9. It achieved a maximum speed of 496.22 km/h (308.33 mph) at Germany’s ATP Automotive Testing [[Papenburg#Transport|Papenburg]]. Later on, it was excluded from the Wikipedia list of production car speed record, due to it being tested only one direction whereas Guinness requires a car to be tested in both direction within one hour for it to be a valid record.<ref>{{Citation |title=List of production car speed records |date=2026-04-29 |work=Wikipedia |url=https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_production_car_speed_records&oldid=1351638481 |access-date=2026-05-13 |language=en}}</ref> <ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-20 |title=Yangwang U9 Xtreme Hits 308 MPH, Becomes World’s Fastest Production Ca |url=https://news.dupontregistry.com/blogs/articles/yangwang-u9-fastest-production-car-in-the-world-308-mph |access-date=2026-05-13 |website=duPont REGISTRY News |language=en}}</ref>It was driven by German track specialist [[Marc Basseng]]. Yangwang also announced a [[Nürburgring Nordschleife]] lap time of 6 minutes 59.157 seconds. Production will be limited to 30 units for global markets.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Leung |first=Adrian |date=2025-09-20 |title=BYD unveils the Yangwang U9 Xtreme, achieving 496.22 km/h, a Nürburgring lap of 6:59.157 |url=https://carnewschina.com/2025/09/20/byd-unveiled-the-yangwang-u9-xtreme-achieving-496-22-km-h-a-nurburgring-lap-of-659-157byd-unveils-the-yangwang-u9-xtreme-achieving-496-22-km-h-a-nurburgring-lap-of-659-157/ |access-date=2025-09-21 |website=CarNewsChina.com |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Bharath |first=Khris |date=2025-09-20 |title=Yangwang U9 Xtreme Hits 308 MPH, Becomes World’s Fastest Production Car |url=https://news.dupontregistry.com/news/yangwang-u9-fastest-production-car-in-the-world-308-mph/ |access-date=2025-09-21 |website=duPont REGISTRY News |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |date=2025-09-20 |title=Yangwang U9 Xtreme hits 308mph, becomes world's fastest ever production car |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/electric/yangwang-u9-xtreme-hits-308mph-becomes-worlds-fastest-ever-production-car |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250921130136/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/electric/yangwang-u9-xtreme-hits-308mph-becomes-worlds-fastest-ever-production-car |archive-date=2025-09-21 |access-date=2025-09-21 |work=Top Gear |language=en |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The top 10 fastest cars in the world in 2026 |url=https://www.carwow.co.uk/blog/fastest-cars-in-the-world |access-date=2026-04-09 |website=carwow.co.uk |language=en}}</ref>

== Media ==
The U9 was added into [[Gran Turismo 7]] as a playable vehicle alongside the [[Renault Twingo]] and the [[Porsche 911 (964)|Porsche 911 Turbo S Leichtbau]] as a part of update 1.69, released in 22 April 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Greer |first=Jordan |date=2025-10-24 |title=The World's Most Powerful Production Car is Coming to Gran Turismo 7 |url=https://www.gtplanet.net/the-worlds-most-powerful-production-car-is-coming-to-gran-turismo-7/ |access-date=2025-10-29 |website=GTPlanet |language=en-US}}</ref>

